  "doc.title": "When Buyers Can't See Quality: Lemons and Insurance · A Hands-On Essay",
  "doc.desc": "Why are used-car lots full of lemons? Why does insurance come with deductibles and medical exams? Why should everyone join public health insurance, and why will a bank refuse a loan rather than raise the rate? Starting from expected value and risk aversion: risk pooling, the market for lemons, the insurance death spiral, deductible menus, signalling, moral hazard, credit rationing and credit scores, with eight hands-on models, ending at the frontier of data-driven pricing, genetic information and enrolment rules.",
  "nav.back": "← Index",
